.TH smi-vscode "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Workstation Command"
.SH NAME
smi-vscode \- Start Visual Studio Code with the Erlang and Elixir environment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-vscode
.RI [ CODE_ARGUMENTS ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Starts Visual Studio Code with the Erlang and Elixir environment set, which an IDE started from the
desktop does not otherwise get: the desktop session does not read the shell profiles, and VS Code
has no setting of its own that reaches every extension and language server it starts.
.PP
The
.I erlang
and
.I elixir
profiles of
.BR smi-profiles-location (1)
are sourced, the same ones
.BR term (1)
uses, so
.BR ERLANG_HOME ,
.BR ELIXIR_HOME ,
.B ELIXIR_ERL_OPTIONS
and
.B PATH
are what a terminal with those profiles has, and VS Code is then started in place of this
command. Every argument is passed to VS Code unchanged.
.PP
The
.I VSCode.desktop
entry of the toolset starts VS Code through this command.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $(smi-profiles-location)/erlang.profile
.TP
.I $(smi-profiles-location)/elixir.profile
The profiles sourced before VS Code starts.
.TP
.I /usr/share/code/code
The VS Code executable, as the vscode package installs it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of VS Code, or non-zero when a profile or VS Code is missing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Open the current directory:
.RS
smi-vscode .
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR code (1),
.BR smi-profiles-location (1),
.BR term (1)
